## Notification System - The Complete Story

### Architecture Decision Tree

```
Q: Why event-driven?
A: Services don't need to know about each other
   Easy to add new notification types later
   Follows SOLID principles

Q: Why async?
A: Don't block HTTP response
   Handle failures independently
   Better user experience

Q: Why WebSocket + Email?
A: Email for permanent record + external notification
   WebSocket for real-time dashboard updates
   Redundancy if one fails
```

### Real-Time Notification Flow

```
Bureau creates new event
          ↓
InscriptionService saves to DB
          ↓
publishEvent(InscriptionCreatedEvent)
          ↓
Event Listener catches event (async)
          ├─→ Send Email (JavaMailSender)
          ├─→ Save notification to DB
          ├─→ Broadcast via WebSocket
          └─→ Trigger actions
          ↓
WebSocket message sent to all connected admin clients
          ↓
Frontend receives message (Observable)
          ↓
Angular UI updates automatically
          ↓
Notification appears (toast/banner)
          ↓
Optional: Sound/browser notification
```

### Why This Approach?

| Aspect | Benefit |
|--------|---------|
| **Event-Driven** | Loosely coupled services |
| **Async** | Non-blocking, better performance |
| **Multi-channel** | Email (persistent) + WebSocket (real-time) |
| **Database Stored** | Audit trail + history |
| **Reactive** | Observable pattern = clean subscriptions |

